<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William I of Württemberg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1781">1781</a>–<a href="/years/1864">1864</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William I was King of Württemberg from 1816 until his death in 1864. He granted a constitution in 1819 that established a two-chamber diet, and promoted farming through the agricultural academy at Hohenheim and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannstatter-wasen">Cannstatter Wasen</a> festival. His long reign consolidated the kingdom created under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> from many former Swabian territories.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-i-of-wurttemberg.jpg" alt="William I of Württemberg" width="320" height="568" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joseph Karl Stieler · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:K%C3%B6nig_Wilhelm_I_W%C3%BCrtt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Born Frederick William Karl at Lüben on 27 September 1781, he was the son of Duke Frederick William Charles of Württemberg and Augusta of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfenbuttel">Wolfenbüttel</a>. His parents' marriage was one of strife, and his mother, who sought sanctuary from it, died in 1788. In 1790 his father and his two sons moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwigsburg-palace">Ludwigsburg Palace</a>, where the boys received a strict education. In 1805 he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a> as Württemberg was forced to ally with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-maurice-de-talleyrand">Talleyrand</a> suggested a coup in which the son would replace his father, Frederick William opposed it, an act regarded as the main reason for his later aversion to Napoleon.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>King Frederick died on 30 October 1816, in the Year Without a Summer, when Württemberg suffered crop failures and famine. His son chose his second name as regnal name, simplified the royal title and the coat of arms, reduced the state flag to black and red, announced an amnesty and replaced most of the ministers. His reforms led the Estates to approve a constitution on 25 September 1819. In his reign of 47 years Württemberg moved from a kingdom of different denominational principalities and a heterogeneous agricultural country to a constitutional state with a common identity. On 28 September 1818 an agricultural festival with horse races and prizes for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>-breeding was held on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannstatter-wasen">Cannstatter Wasen</a>, the origin of the Cannstatter Volksfest.</p><p>In foreign policy he imagined a third major German power beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, formed of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a> and Württemberg. The plan never succeeded. After poor harvests in 1846 and 1847 liberal demands grew, and in March 1848 he reinstated the liberal press law of 1817 and agreed to a liberal government under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-romer">Friedrich Römer</a>. He was the only German monarch forced to recognise the Frankfurt Constitution of 1848. After the revolution failed he followed reactionary policies. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a> he pushed for the neutrality of the German Confederation, and on 27 September 1857 he organised a meeting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a> between Napoléon III and Tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-ii-of-russia">Alexander II</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He died on 25 June 1864 at Rosenstein Castle in Bad Cannstatt and was buried in the Württemberg Mausoleum. In old age European princes often sought his advice, and his biographer Paul Sauer called him the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nestor">Nestor</a> among the princes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. He married three times. By his second wife, Grand Duchess Catherine Pavlovna of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, he had two daughters, one of whom, Sophie, married King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-iii-of-the-netherlands">William III of the Netherlands</a>. By his third wife, Pauline, he had a son, Charles, who succeeded him as king.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “William I of Württemberg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Cannstatter Wasen, one of Germany's largest folk festivals, began in 1818 as an agricultural fair founded by King <b>William I</b> after the famine of 1816–1817.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wurttemberg">Kingdom of Württemberg · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Lubin, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. Died in Rosenstein Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.4,16.2&amp;year=1834" aria-label="Lubin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4917 384 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of William I of Württemberg"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5367 654h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5367 654h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.4,16.2&amp;year=1834">What was here in 1834? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
